  "tags": ["heap", "sliding-window", "sorting", "greedy"],
  "companies": ["Google", "Amazon", "Snap"],
  "editorial_md": "### Intuition\nKeep one \"current\" element from each list. The range they span is `[smallest current, largest current]`. The only way to possibly shrink it is to advance the list that holds the **smallest** current element — advancing any other list can only raise the maximum.\n\n### Algorithm\nPush the first element of every list into a min-heap and track the largest pushed value `hi`. Repeatedly pop the minimum `lo`, compare `[lo, hi]` with the best range, then push the next element from the same list (updating `hi`). Stop when a list runs out: from then on no range can cover it.\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef smallestRange(nums):\n    heap = [(lst[0], i, 0) for i, lst in enumerate(nums)]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    hi = max(lst[0] for lst in nums)\n    best = [heap[0][0], hi]\n    while True:\n        lo, i, j = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if hi - lo < best[1] - best[0] or (hi - lo == best[1] - best[0] and lo < best[0]):\n            best = [lo, hi]\n        if j + 1 == len(nums[i]):\n            return best\n        nxt = nums[i][j + 1]\n        hi = max(hi, nxt)\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (nxt, i, j + 1))\n```\n\n### Complexity\nO(N log k) time for N total values across k lists, O(k) extra space.\n\n### Alternative\nMerge all values (tagged with their list), sort them, and slide a window that contains every list at least once — O(N log N).",
